  "step_3": "The decision reason requires (1) computing an aspiration level as the mean of all distinct outcomes (2333.33), and (2) comparing the probabilities that each lottery yields an outcome above this level. Lottery A has a 25% chance (3000 > 2333.33), Lottery B has 20% (4000 > 2333.33), so the reason selects A. The individual also chooses A, but their justification is based solely on the higher probability of winning any money (25% vs 20%) and a willingness to accept a lower amount for higher odds. This reflects a 'satisficing' or 'probability maximization for non-zero gain' logic, not a comparison to an aspiration level based on the mean of all outcomes. The individual does not reference or imply the calculation of such a mean, nor do they discuss differences between outcomes and this benchmark. While both the reason and the individual prefer Lottery A, the underlying rationale differs: the reason depends on outcome-aspiration comparisons, while the individual focuses on the chance of winning something versus nothing. There is no evidence the individual computed or used the aspiration level. Therefore, the match in choice is coincidental, not indicative of the same reasoning process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

